ance audits  in place there so go run the lighthouse  from the chrome developer tools it's  also supported on webpagetest and that  will give you some insight into you know  are you just sending way too much code  down to your users if you are it'll tell  you if javascript boot up time is an  issue and it'll also give you a few  ideas  around things that you can do to reduce  that amount of script now the reason why  javascript has got such a cost is that  there are a number of different steps a  browser has to take any order to process  it like if you know if you're loading up  a web page that JavaScript first of all  needs to be fetched over a network then  needs to be parsed by the browser  parsing can take a very long time a  slower CPU it has to be compiled and  then executed for many sites if you're  shipping Meg's and Meg's and mountains  worth of JavaScript down to users that  can take seconds and seconds of time and  that's not so uncommon in the HTTP  archive we've seen at the 90th  percentile sites are actually sending  more than a megabyte of JavaScript and  that's gzipped  and minified yeah so that's a lot of  code that's a lot of code that's so  that's yeah as you said that's minified  in gzip code we're saying you know  uncompressed is probably 2 or 3 times  that so a lot of people are you know  having a browser deal with Meg's of  JavaScript literally then has to process  just great yeah but the good news is  that the median is only only 400  kilobytes it's still a huge amount of  code but it's not in a megabytes luckily  so what guidance would you give to  developers to use JavaScript responsibly  throw it all out  no don't you can't you can't throw it  all so what I recommend doing is only  ship the code that you use your needs  for the current page or the current  route very often what sites will do is  they'll bundle all of their JavaScript  into one big monolithic glob and ship  that down instead of doing that what if  you broke it up in smaller pieces so  just rip the code needed for the current  page once that's fetched you can use  techniques like prefetching pre-caching  using a serviceworker  to go and start fetching in lazily  loading in the rest the code needed and  the benefit of that is the user can  start interacting with the spirit the  experience very early on and you know if  they go and start interacting with the  rest of the pages on the site you've had  an opportunity to you know get that code  into the cache and maybe a little bit  quicker so general try to do that the  technique that you'd use for that is  called code splitting it's a check  supported by lots of tools things like  wet pack and parcel support it it's  possible to code split using you know  things like react and view and angularjs  pre-act most popular frameworks support  this I would love to get to a point  where more frameworks are code splitting  by default or rather just encouraging  you to kind of shape your codes so  you're not going to be bundling  everything into a single file but code  splitting is the first big one  I think another thing you can do is take  advantage of techniques like tree  shaking so tree shaking although it  sounds a little violence is this idea of  removing unused you know imports in your  code so if I'm gonna be importing in a  library like a you know some piece of  you know UI maybe I'll mom importing in  bootstrap I'm importing is something  else I probably don't need everything  that it provides so tree shaking is this  idea of only including the imports that  you're actually using and stripping away  everything else to reduce that overall  amount of script and the experience  there are lots of other things we notice  like most of the sites that I trace will  include polyfills that are probably not  needed for modern browsers a common one  is people still polyfill JavaScript  promises you know promises have been  well supported for a number of years I  promise but I think that you know aud